 ## Overview
-Importance Ray Sampling is a technique used in neural volume rendering to refine the distribution of sample points along a ray [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [NeRF Importance Sampling](1565-nerf-importance-sampling.md) by explicitly defining the procedural steps for binning, smoothing, and stratified sampling within a discrete partition of the ray [S1, unofficial]. It is a sampling strategy that uses initial density estimates (weights) to sample more densely from regions expected to have objects [S1, unofficial].
+Importance Ray Sampling is a technique used in neural volume rendering to refine the distribution of sample points along a ray [S1, unofficial]. It uses initial density estimates (weights) to sample more densely from regions expected to have objects [S1, unofficial]. The method partitions the ray into coarse bins, computes a probability mass function from the weights, samples bin indices, and interpolates within those bins [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs:**
-- `volume_range`: The spatial extent of the volume along the ray, partitioned into coarse bins.
-- `self.weights`: Raw density or opacity estimates for each coarse bin, derived from a coarse model.
-- `self.alpha`: A small scalar added to weights to prevent zero-probability bins.
-- `blur_pool`: A boolean flag indicating whether to apply smoothing filters.
-- `n_samples`: The number of fine sample points to generate.
